2025-12-20 18:18:38,296 - __main__ - INFO - Content: ```java
public int minSwaps(int[] data) {
    int onesCount = 0;
    for (int num : data)
        if (num == 1)
            ++onesCount;

    if (onesCount % 2 != 0)
        return -1; // Can't form groups

    onesCount /= 2;
    long totalOneBits = 0;
    int result = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < onesCount * 2; ++i) {
        while (totalOneBits & (1 << (onesCount + 1)) && i < onesCount * two) {
            ++result;
            ++totalOneBits ^= (1 << (ones_count));
        }
        totalOneBits |= ((1 << (ones_count + 1)) >> i);
    }

    return result;
}
```


Explanation:
First, count how many '1's exist in the input array (`count_ones`). If it has an odd value, then we can’t group them all together as they will always be separated by at least one zero.

Next, divide `count_ones` by 2 because each pair consists of two `'1'`.

Initialize variables:

- `result`: To store the answer
- `total_one_bits`: A bitwise variable which stores bits set to 1 from the first half of the grouped pairs
- `ones_two`: The index where the second part starts

Loop through the numbers from 0 up to twice the length of the first half of the grouping:

For every iteration:
1. Check whether there exists any bit set in the current bitmask `(1<<ones_count)` so far and its position is less than `ones_two`.
2. Increment `result` when found.
3. Set the corresponding bit in the mask (`total_one_bits`) equal to the complement of the current bit (`mask ^ (bit | 1 << (ones_count)))`.

After looping, return the final result.
