#include <vector>
using namespace std;

int minDifference(vector<int>& nums) {
    sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());
    int result = INT_MAX;
    
    for(int i=0; i<nums.size(); ++i)
        result = min(result, abs(i%7 - ((nums[nums.size()-1]/(double)(nums.size())) % 7)) + 1);

    return result;
}
```


Explanation:
First, we need to understand what's happening with modulo operations here. We're taking each element from the array and applying some arithmetic operation based on its index in the sorted array.

We start by sorting the input array. Then, for each value at index 'i' in this sorted array, we want to calculate how many times it appears in the range [0, num/7]. This can be done using the floor division operator `%`, which gives us the remainder when dividing by 'num'. Since our original data set contains only one occurrence of each unique integer within the bounds described above, we just take the remainder as part of the calculation.

Finally, we add 1 to the calculated distance plus 1 to get the minimum possible distance between any pair of elements equal to the current value. To make sure we have the correct answer, we compare these values across different elements in the sorted list and choose the smallest one among them.

This approach ensures that we minimize the maximum difference while satisfying the condition required by the problem statement. In C++ code, similar logic would involve iterating through the sorted array, calculating distances accordingly, and keeping track of the minimal differences found so far.
